NAME
    cd - change the current directory

SYNOPSIS
    cd [dir]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes the process-wide current directory - the same one 'pwd' reads
    and every relative path elsewhere in this shell (redirection, 'ls', 'mv',
    'rm', 'source', 'wc <path>', ...) resolves against. No argument goes to
    the current account's own home directory (see 'whoami'/'hier') - real
    $HOME semantics, scoped to this shell's own tree. A path starting with
    '/' means THIS shell's own root, not the real OS filesystem root -
    'cd /SourceLogs' works from anywhere, the same way a real chroot makes '/'
    mean the chroot directory (see 'hier'). Walled to the project root no
    matter how it's spelled - no amount of 'cd ..', a leading '/', or a long
    '../../..' chain can leave it; this is a deliberate walled garden against
    accidents, not a security boundary. A directory name with spaces doesn't
    need quoting ('cd My Folder' works) - cd only ever takes one path, so
    everything after it is treated as that path literally; quoting
    ('cd "My Folder"') also works if you prefer it.

EXAMPLES
    cd EmuSen.DianaSh
    cd /SourceLogs
    cd My Folder
    cd
